class:: WsMap
summary:: Wavesets computed from the previous waveset through an iterated map
related:: Classes/WsGen, Classes/WsGendy, Classes/Dmap
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic

description::
Each waveset is computed from the one before it. A state x between 0 and 1 is stepped once per waveset through a nonlinear map. x sets the waveset length on an exponential scale between code::lo:: and code::hi::, a second stream sets the skew, and x can scale the peak. For the two-dimensional maps the second stream is the map's own y; for the others it is the change in x since the last waveset. Fixed points give a steady pitch, periodic orbits give repeating patterns of waveset lengths, and chaotic orbits give noisy, rough streams.

All inputs except code::init:: are read once per waveset, so demand-rate inputs advance one value per waveset.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: r
Map parameter, 0 to 1, scaled to each map's full range. Higher values move towards chaos.

For the logistic map: a single fixed point below 0.75, period 2 from 0.75, period 4 from about 0.862, chaos from about 0.892, with a period 3 window from about 0.958.

For the tent map: decays to 0 below 0.5, chaotic above.

For the sine map: like the logistic map, slightly earlier: period 2 from about 0.75, chaos from about 0.87.

For the circle map: short locked periods alternate with irregular stretches as r rises, and above about 0.6 it is mostly irregular.

For the standard map: quasi-periodic drift below about 0.4, islands and chaos alternating up to about 0.65, chaotic above 0.7.

For the Ikeda map: a fixed point below about 0.45, period 2 from 0.45, period 4 near 0.7, chaos from about 0.75.

See also link::Classes/Dmap::, which steps the same maps at demand rate.

argument:: lo
Waveset frequency in Hz when x is 0.

argument:: hi
Waveset frequency in Hz when x is 1.

argument:: amp
Peak amplitude.

argument:: shape
Half-cycle shape: 0 sine, 1 triangle, 2 square, 3 spike. Fractional values morph between neighbours.

argument:: map
0 logistic, 1 tent, 2 sine, 3 circle, 4 standard, 5 Ikeda. 4 and 5 are two-dimensional.

argument:: skewDepth
How strongly the second stream moves the middle zero crossing. 0 keeps the half-cycles equal.

argument:: ampDepth
How strongly x lowers the peak, 0 to 1. At 1 the shortest wavesets are the quietest.

argument:: init
Starting value of x, 0 to 1. Read once when the synth starts.

argument:: balance
Scales down the larger half-cycle so both halves enclose the same area, which removes the DC offset that skew and uneven halves produce, 0 (off) to 1 (equal area). Zero crossings and the peak stay where they are; the level drops as the halves become more uneven.

argument:: hyst
Half-width of a dead band on code::r::. The effective r only moves when code::r:: pushes past it, so sweeping up and back down leaves the map in different places, and small fluctuations of code::r:: are ignored.

argument:: mul

argument:: add

method:: arTrig
Same as link::#*ar:: with one more argument, and returns code::[sig, trig]::. code::trig:: is a single-sample 1 at the start of the first waveset and then of every code::every::-th waveset (repeats count), so the waveset clock can drive other UGens.

argument:: every
Wavesets between triggers. Read each time the trigger fires, so a demand-rate input sets the number of wavesets until the next one.

examples::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(MouseX.kr(0.7, 1), 60, 1200, 0.3) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(0.8, 100, 800, 0.3) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(0.875, 100, 800, 0.3) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(0.958, 100, 800, 0.3) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(1, 40, 4000, 0.3, 0, 0, 2, 0.8) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(MouseX.kr(0.5, 1), 50, 2000, 0.3, MouseY.kr(0, 3), 1) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(MouseX.kr(0.6, 1), 30, 900, 0.3, 1, 2, 3) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(MouseX.kr(0, 1), 80, 600, 0.3, 0, 3, MouseY.kr(0, 4)) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ [WsMap.ar(0.97, 60, 1200, 0.3, init: 0.3), WsMap.ar(0.97, 60, 1200, 0.3, init: 0.3001)] }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(Dstutter(200, Dseq([0.8, 0.875, 0.95, 1], inf)), 70, 1400, 0.3, 1, 0, 1.5) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
(
{
	var r = Dseq([Dseries(0.7, 0.001, 300), Dseries(1, -0.001, 300)], inf);
	WsMap.ar(r, 50, 1500, 0.3, 0, 0, 2) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var r = LFNoise1.kr(0.1).range(0.85, 1);
	WsMap.ar(r, 40, LFNoise1.kr(0.07).exprange(300, 5000), 0.3, LFNoise2.kr(0.3).range(0, 3), 0, LFNoise1.kr(0.2).range(0, 4), 0.5) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(0.99, 20, 200, 0.4, 3, 1, 1) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(0.9, 200, 6000, 0.25, 2, 2, 2, 1) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
(
{
	Splay.ar(Array.fill(4, { |i|
		WsMap.ar(0.88 + (i * 0.035), 60 * (i + 1), 900 * (i + 1), 0.35, i, i, 2, 0.5)
	}))
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var map = Dstutter(Dwhite(50, 400), Dseq([0, 1, 2, 3], inf));
	WsMap.ar(0.97, 50, 1600, 0.3, Dbrown(0, 3, 0.2), map, 2, 0.6) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var src = WsMap.ar(MouseX.kr(0.7, 1), 30, 600, 0.4, 1, 0, 3);
	LeakDC.ar(RLPF.ar(src, MouseY.kr(100, 8000, 1), 0.15)) * 0.25 ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var sig, trig;
	#sig, trig = WsMap.arTrig(MouseX.kr(0.7, 1), 2, 20, 0);
	Ringz.ar(trig, TExpRand.ar(300, 3000, trig), 0.1, 0.2) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var trig = WsMap.arTrig(0.875, 3, 12, 0)[1];
	var env = Decay2.ar(trig, 0.003, 0.15);
	WsGen.ar(Latch.ar(TExpRand.ar(60, 400, trig), trig), env * 0.4, 1) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var sig, trig;
	#sig, trig = WsMap.arTrig(0.97, 50, 1200, 0.6, 0, 0, 2, 0, balance: 1, every: Dseq([16, 8, 4, 32], inf));
	Pan2.ar(sig, TRand.ar(-0.8, 0.8, trig))
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var sig, trig;
	#sig, trig = WsMap.arTrig(LFNoise1.kr(0.1).range(0.85, 1), 40, 900, 0.3, 1, 0, 1.5, every: 32);
	sig + PinkNoise.ar(Decay2.ar(trig, 0.001, 0.03) * 0.3) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
{ WsMap.ar(1, 40, 4000, 0.4, 0, 0, MouseX.kr(0, 4), 0.5, balance: MouseY.kr(0, 1)) ! 2 }.play;
